<TopicPage slug="subjects/radiation-therapy/foundational/therapy-physics-and-the-linac">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Radiation Therapy. It assumes comfortable high-school
    physics — energy, electrons, the idea of radiation — and builds everything else. The
    mathematics never rises past multiplication and division, but every number is treated
    as if a life depended on it, because one does.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace a linac's beam from electron gun to patient, naming each stage's job;</li>
    <li>explain why dose peaks below the skin, and what that buys the patient;</li>
    <li>work a simplified monitor-unit calculation, the discipline's signature sum;</li>
    <li>read a percentage depth–dose curve — the build-up region, the peak at d<sub>max</sub>, the slow fall;</li>
    <li>say why treatment comes as small daily fractions rather than one large dose;</li>
    <li>explain why the field treats physics as a safety discipline first.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit is worth naming at the start: the beam cannot be seen, felt or taken back.
    Everything in this lesson — the redundant checks, the verified calculations, the daily
    quality assurance — exists because physics without verification is dangerous physics.
  </p>

  <h2>The paradox at the heart of the treatment</h2>
  <p>
    Ionising radiation damages every cell it passes through — tumour or healthy, there is
    no chemistry that distinguishes them. So how can it cure? The answer is the field's
    founding insight: the cure comes not from a different <em>substance</em> but from
    <em>distribution</em>. Stack the dose where the tumour is, spread it thin everywhere
    else, and exploit biology so that healthy cells repair between doses better than
    tumour cells do. A diagnostic X-ray aims to deliver the smallest useful dose; therapy
    deliberately delivers thousands of times more — and gets away with it only through
    geometry, timing and verification.
  </p>
  <p>
    The machine at the centre of that trick is the <strong>linear accelerator</strong> —
    the linac — a device the size of a small room that manufactures a precisely shaped
    beam of high-energy photons and swings it around the patient from any angle.
    Understanding the linac means understanding the trade's whole foundation: how the beam
    is made, how it behaves once it enters tissue (where dose peaks, how it falls, how it
    scatters), and how those behaviours are calculated into a treatment plan. Every number
    a radiation therapist reads on a treatment console — monitor units, energy, field
    size, depth — is an output of the physics on this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The five cards below preview the whole machine and its schedule, from microwave gun to
    fractionated dose.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from microwave gun to fractionated dose" widget="equationStep" config={linacCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps divide into three jobs. Steps 1–2
    <em>make</em> the beam (electrons, then photons); steps 3–4 <em>shape and aim</em> it;
    step 5 spreads it out in <em>time</em> — the one step that is biology rather than
    engineering. And notice what is missing from the cards: any mention of a radioactive
    material. The beam is manufactured on demand, which will matter later.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the treatment is a distribution problem — a lot of dose where
    the tumour is, a little everywhere else, divided into recoverable daily pieces. Now we
    open the machine that produces it.
  </p>

  <h2>Linear accelerator operation</h2>
  <p>
    A linac is a chain of specialists, each stage handing a more refined beam to the next.
    It begins with the <strong>electron gun</strong>, which injects electrons into a
    <strong>standing-wave accelerating waveguide</strong> — a copper tube in which
    microwave energy (nearly three billion cycles per second) sets up electric fields that
    surf the electrons forward, accelerating them to within a hair of light speed and to
    energies of roughly 6–20 megavolts (MV). A bending magnet then sweeps the beam around
    a corner — which doubles as a filter, because only electrons with the chosen energy
    can make the turn.
  </p>
  <p>
    What happens next defines the machine's two modes. For <strong>photon (X-ray)
    therapy</strong>, the fast electrons slam into a tungsten target, and the sudden
    deceleration converts their kinetic energy into a flood of high-energy X-ray photons
    (<strong>bremsstrahlung</strong>, German for "braking radiation") that emerges roughly
    forward. For <strong>electron therapy</strong>, the target is retracted and the
    electron beam itself exits — useful for shallow targets like skin lesions and lymph
    nodes. The raw photon beam is forward-peaked and uneven, so a conical
    <strong>flattening filter</strong> evens it across the field — or, in modern
    flattening-filter-free designs, the profile is accepted raw in exchange for much
    higher dose rates. Collimation follows: adjustable <strong>jaws</strong> set the
    rectangular field, and the <strong>multileaf collimator</strong> — up to 120 tungsten
    fingers, each up to a centimetre wide at the patient, driven individually — sculpts
    the beam to the tumour's cross-section and moves live during treatment to paint an
    intensity pattern. The whole treatment head rides a <strong>gantry</strong> rotating a
    full circle around the couch, so beams converge on the tumour from any angle.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a linac stores radiation, like a radioactive source
  in a cabinet." Unlike a cobalt-60 unit — whose gamma-emitting source never switches off
  and must be shielded around the clock — a linac is off when it is off. It manufactures
  photons only while powered and holds no radioactive material. That property shapes
  everything from machine servicing to decommissioning.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "after an external-beam treatment the patient is
  radioactive." They are not. The photons pass through and are gone; no radioactive
  material is left in the body, and a patient who has just received photon therapy poses
  no dose to anyone they hug. (Brachytherapy and unsealed radioactive sources, which do
  call for precautions, are different techniques met in a later unit — the confusion
  between them and external-beam therapy is common and worth heading off now.)</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the linac is a photon factory with a sculpting head: make
    electrons fast, convert them to X-rays, shape the field with collimators, aim from any
    angle. Next, the numbers — how a prescription in grays becomes a setting in monitor
    units.
  </p>

  <h2>Dose calculation fundamentals</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Dose</strong> is measured in the <strong>gray</strong> (Gy): one joule of
    absorbed energy per kilogram of tissue. Typical prescriptions — 60 Gy for a breast,
    70 Gy for a prostate — are delivered in fractions of about 2 Gy, and the treatment
    machine tracks that dose through <strong>monitor units</strong> (MUs), read from a
    calibrated ionisation chamber in the machine's head that counts the beam actually
    delivered. Converting a prescription into monitor units is the discipline's signature
    calculation, and it multiplies a handful of measured beam factors.
  </p>
  <p>
    A simplified worked case shows the anatomy of the calculation. Suppose a plan
    prescribes 2 Gy (200 cGy) per fraction to a tumour 8 cm deep, and the machine's
    calibration says one monitor unit delivers about 1 cGy at the depth of maximum dose
    (d<sub>max</sub>) for a 10 × 10 cm field. The percentage depth–dose (PDD) at 8 cm for
    a 6 MV beam is about 70 per cent, so each MU deposits roughly 0.70 cGy at the tumour
    depth. The required monitor units are therefore 200 ÷ 0.70 ≈ 286 MU. Real plans add
    correction factors for field size, inverse-square distance effects, tissue
    inhomogeneities (lung transmits dose better than muscle; bone shields it) and — for
    modern techniques — the intensity map the leaves traced. The method is the point:
    every factor is measured, every multiplication is checked, and the final MU count is
    verified by a second person or an independent calculation before the beam is turned
    on.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three physical rules underneath every calculation are worth memorising as
    behaviours. Dose <strong>attenuates</strong> exponentially with depth as photons are
    absorbed and scattered. Dose falls with the <strong>inverse square</strong> of
    distance from the source (double the distance, quarter the dose rate) — the reason
    every 5 cm of patient geometry matters. And dose <strong>builds up</strong> below the
    surface before it decays — which is not a nuisance but the design feature the next
    section exploits.
  </p>

  <h2>Beam characteristics: the depth–dose curve</h2>
  <p>
    A megavoltage beam's dose versus depth — its percentage depth–dose curve — is the
    characteristic profile planners live inside. Dose starts low at the very surface,
    builds over the first millimetres to centimetres as secondary electrons scatter
    forward, peaks at d<sub>max</sub> (about 1.5 cm for 6 MV, about 2.5 cm at 10 MV and
    deeper still at higher energies), then attenuates slowly. The consequences are the
    trade's favourite facts. <strong>Skin sparing</strong>: the dose peak lies
    centimetres deep, so the skin itself is largely spared — a huge advantage over the
    orthovoltage era, and the reason bolus material must sometimes be placed deliberately
    when the skin <em>is</em> the target. <strong>Multi-beam stacking</strong>: because
    the beam attenuates slowly, a single beam delivers more dose to the entrance path
    than to the tumour; aimed through the body from several angles, each beam's low-dose
    entry and exit paths spread dose everywhere, while the peaks and central beams
    overlap at the target — the geometric heart of conformal therapy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="percentage depth–dose for a 6 MV beam" widget="plotter" config={pdCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve does two contrary things in sequence. It
    <em>rises</em> over the first centimetre and a half — that is the build-up region,
    and the gap between the surface dose and the peak is the skin sparing — and then it
    <em>decays</em>, slowly: even 30 cm deep, roughly a fifth of the peak dose remains.
    The slow tail is why one beam alone cannot safely concentrate dose, and why several
    beams from different angles are stacked so their high-dose regions overlap at the
    tumour while their entrance paths stay scattered.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Energy choice</strong> then becomes a matching exercise: higher energies push
    d<sub>max</sub> deeper and attenuate less (better for deep tumours), while lower
    energies keep dose more contained near the entrance (kinder to the healthy tissue
    between the surface and a shallow target). <strong>Electron beams</strong> obey a
    different rule with its own neat arithmetic: their dose is deposited within a
    shallow, fairly sharp range — roughly E/3 centimetres of therapeutic depth for E
    megaelectronvolts — so a 9 MeV electron beam treats about 3 cm deep and stops,
    sparing what lies beneath. And <strong>field edges</strong> carry their own
    character: the penumbra, the zone of partial dose at the boundary, is narrowed by
    collimator design and by placing the patient far from the source, and minimising it
    is what lets planners protect a spinal cord sitting millimetres from a target.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a more powerful machine — higher energy — is always
  the better one." Energy is a matching decision, not a ranking. Higher energies penetrate
  deep tumours more efficiently but push the peak dose deeper, which can <em>worsen</em>
  the dose to healthy tissue en route to a shallow target; 6 MV machines treat the great
  majority of cases. The best beam is the one whose depth–dose curve fits the patient's
  geometry.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> radiation "burns through" the skin to reach the
  tumour, so skin damage is inevitable. The build-up region exists precisely to avoid
  this — surface dose problems in modern therapy are usually a sign of a deliberate
  choice (bolus, oblique beams) rather than an unavoidable cost.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the beam's behaviour in tissue is predictable — build-up,
    peak, slow decay, inverse square — and every treatment decision is a way of exploiting
    those curves. What remains is the fourth dimension: time.
  </p>

  <h2>Fractionation: why the dose comes in small daily pieces</h2>
  <p>
    Why not deliver 60 Gy at once? Because the tumour's advantage over normal tissue is
    not in surviving the beam but in <em>repairing between beams</em> — less well. Split
    the dose into daily fractions of about 2 Gy and a race begins overnight: normal cells
    mend their damaged DNA and resume work; tumour cells repair worse, die off in the
    days after each fraction, and expose formerly oxygen-starved, radiation-resistant
    survivors as the mass shrinks. Thirty to thirty-five fractions later, the tumour has
    accumulated unrecoverable damage while the healthy tissue — given its nightly head
    start — has kept pace. The prescription on the console is therefore a biological
    schedule as much as a physical quantity, which is why changing the dose per fraction
    changes everything downstream, as you will see in Going deeper.
  </p>

  <h2>From blocks to leaves: how field shaping evolved</h2>
  <p>
    The multileaf collimator's precision is recent history, and its evolution teaches how
    the physics earns its keep. Early therapy shaped fields by hand: <em>custom blocks</em>
    — dense alloy cast to the tumour's projected shape, wedged into the beam's path for
    each treatment — which worked but consumed hours per patient, allowed only one static
    shape per field, and exposed staff to daily handling. The first multileaf collimators
    automated the blocking with motorised tungsten leaves; modern ones go further, moving
    <em>during</em> the treatment so that dozens of changing field shapes deliver an
    intensity pattern no static block could paint. The clinical payoff is measurable:
    tumour concavities can be dosed while a nearby spinal cord or parotid gland is
    protected, and treatment times fell from tens of minutes of fiddling to minutes of
    automated delivery. The lesson generalises: in this discipline, every mechanical
    refinement of the beam is a way of buying normal tissue a smaller dose — and every
    fraction of a gray matters, because the doses that cure tumours are the same doses
    that hurt healthy tissue when misdelivered.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In one sentence each: what do the electron gun, the tungsten target and the multileaf collimator each contribute to the beam?</li>
    <li>A 6 MV beam's dose peaks at about 1.5 cm. Why is that peak an advantage, and what must planners add when the skin itself is the target?</li>
    <li>A plan prescribes 200 cGy at a depth where each monitor unit delivers 0.50 cGy. How many monitor units?</li>
    <li>Why does the standard course spread 60 Gy over about thirty 2 Gy fractions instead of delivering it in one session?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The gun supplies the electrons; the target converts
    their kinetic energy into X-ray photons (bremsstrahlung); the collimator's tungsten
    leaves sculpt the field to the tumour's cross-section — and move during treatment to
    modulate intensity. (2) The peak sits below the surface, so the skin receives the
    build-up dose rather than the maximum — skin sparing. When the skin is the target,
    bolus material is added to pull d<sub>max</sub> back up to the surface. (3) 200 ÷ 0.50
    = 400 MU — the same division the worked example made, with the numbers exchanged.
    (4) Fractionation exploits the repair differential: normal tissue mends sublethal
    damage between daily fractions better than tumours do, while shrinking tumours
    reoxygenate and become more sensitive — one big dose would burn normal tissue and
    lose those biological advantages.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Radiation damages all cells alike; cure comes from distribution — dose stacked at the tumour, spread elsewhere, and divided over time.</li>
    <li>The linac manufactures its beam: microwave-accelerated electrons, a tungsten target for photons (or direct electrons for shallow therapy), collimators and a rotating gantry to shape and aim it.</li>
    <li>A linac holds no radioactive source — off is off — and external-beam patients leave containing no radioactivity.</li>
    <li>Dose is measured in grays and delivered via monitor units; the signature calculation multiplies measured factors and is verified twice.</li>
    <li>The depth–dose curve has three acts: build-up, peak at d<sub>max</sub>, slow exponential decay — the basis of skin sparing and multi-beam stacking.</li>
    <li>Electron beams deposit dose in roughly E/3 centimetres — depth you can dial in.</li>
    <li>Fractionation (about 2 Gy daily) is biology: normal tissue repairs overnight; tumours accumulate damage, reoxygenate and die.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson moves from the beam to the patient: simulation and immobilisation,
    target volumes and organs at risk, and how 3D and intensity-modulated plans are
    built on the physics you now know. Patient care, site-specific techniques and — at
    the top of the programme — the quality-assurance and safety culture that audits every
    number on this page follow. The linac you have just met is the permanent fixture
    behind all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fractionation is biology, not scheduling.</strong> Radiobiology's five Rs —
    repair, reassortment, repopulation, reoxygenation, intrinsic radiosensitivity —
    explain why the standard 2 Gy/day schedule works, and also why stereotactic regimes
    delivering huge doses (18–24 Gy) in one to five fractions required new technology —
    tighter positioning, steeper dose fall-off, image guidance — to stay safe: the
    fractionation biology changes when the dose per fraction does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Modern delivery multiplies the physics.</strong> Intensity-modulated therapy
    (IMRT/VMAT) uses the moving leaves to vary dose rate across the field hundreds of
    times per beam, carving high dose around organs-at-risk with concavities no single
    field could produce. Image-guided radiation therapy (IGRT) adds daily on-board
    imaging — cone-beam CT or marker tracking — because tumours move with breathing and
    patients change day to day; sub-millimetre positioning is now routine.
    Flattening-filter-free beams trade beam uniformity for very high dose rates, which is
    what makes stereotactic treatments short enough for patients to hold still. Each
    layer is the same linac with a sharper question asked of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The team and the safety net are part of the physics.</strong> Radiation
    oncology is a triangle: the radiation oncologist prescribes, the medical physicist
    plans, calibrates and checks, and the radiation therapist delivers daily — positioning
    the patient to sub-centimetre accuracy and running the pre-treatment verification
    checklist. The profession's safety culture (independent MU verification, machine QA
    with daily, monthly and annual tolerances, incident-learning systems) exists because
    the beam cannot be seen, felt or taken back — and because history's radiotherapy
    accidents were, almost uniformly, physics errors that a verification layer would have
    caught. Learning the linac is learning both halves of that sentence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quality assurance gives the physics its daily numbers.</strong> Machine QA
    runs at three cadences: daily checks (a few minutes — output constancy, laser
    alignment, safety interlocks and door) before the first patient; monthly checks (a
    full set of output, energy and flatness measurements against tolerance); and annual
    comprehensive calibration by the physicist with a water-tank scanning system that maps
    the entire beam. Tolerances are tight by design — output, for instance, must stay
    within about 2 per cent of baseline, because a systematic delivery error repeated
    over thirty fractions compounds into a real dose error. The therapist's role in the
    same chain: the pre-treatment checks (interlocks, imaging-couch calibration), in-vivo
    dosimetry on selected patients, and the discipline of never treating on a machine
    whose morning check failed. QA is the least glamorous page of the physics chapter and
    the one the profession would name if asked which keeps patients safest.
  </p>

